Verdict: PASS. I wrote it to session_plan/eval_task_1.md. I didn't run the binary; my evidence is the diff, the task's test rows (build and tests were already green), the issue comments and the commit message.

• The fix: a new helper, rm_wide_target, now flags a recursive rm aimed at any top-level folder. It matches on the path's form (one component under /), not a list, so the made-up /data is caught too. It also flags the working folder written as ./, $PWD, ${PWD}, $(pwd) or  pwd . Users see the same "Destructive command" warning that rm -rf . already gives.
• Near-misses: the scan stops at &&, ;, | and redirects, so rm -rf ./target, rm -rf /tmp/x, rm -rf build && cd /app and rm -rf build > /out.log still pass. The hard block (on /, ~ and $HOME) is unchanged, and the tests check that.
• Wiring: the helper is called in the same diff, the new tests file is declared from safety.rs, and the recorded size in tests/module_size.rs moved to 4856 to match the 41 added lines. No NEUTERED marker is left in either file.
